  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 232 PM MDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\nSynoptically, the ridge that has been in place over the Four \nCorners region is still likely to stay nearly stationary over \nthe next week. Throughout the week, Guidance does show some \nindication of shortwaves moving through the region. \n\nOverall, high temperatures look to \"cool\" down to the mid to \nhigh 90s for the majority of the week. Winds for the week look \nto remain calm to breezy, with relative humidity values in the \n30-60% range. \n\nMoving to the precipation chances this week: starting with \nTuesday, Guidance mainly the NAM and the RRFS shows a boundary \nover the southern part of the CO/KS border. With this boundary, \nmoisture advection from the south and will move northward of \nover the CWA. Winds will mainly be from the east, causing \nupslope flow and will help influence shower/storm production. \nPoPs are in the 30-50% range, and the probability of exceeding \n0.10\" of precipitation is in the 15-30% for areas west of KS \nHwy 27. However, one thing to note is that some models do not \nshow this boundary or show a weaker version on the boundary. \nGiven this is roughly four days out, it will be interesting to \nsee how Guidance will trend with the boundary and its location \nfor precipitation chances.\n\nOnce again, Wednesday has similar precipitation chances with \nPoPs in the 20-50% range. Currently, Guidance looks to have \nanother boundary in place, but there is a fair amount of \ndisagreement on where the boundary will be located. Given we \nhave moisture advection from the south, this will help the \nprecipitation chances. \n\nLooking at the rest of the week, each afternoon/evening does \nhave around a 10-20% range for PoPs. These chances will be \ndependent on how Tuesday and Wednesday pan out. While a fair \namount can change, there is a signal that precipitation chances \ndo return to the area.",
